{"t":1790844885,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"67cf16b8-4b6e-4ec9-a95b-aa1c8f738637","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-01T08:00:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 010800\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n400 AM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nPatchy fog is developing this morning. Timing and coverage of\nrain continue to subtly shift through the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) After patchy morning fog, temperatures will remain above \n  normal through Friday.\n\n- 2) A stalling cold front may bring rain at times from Friday\n  through Sunday.\n\n- 3) Cooler and drier weather early next week with potential for\n  frost and freeze west of the Blue Ridge Mountains.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...After patchy morning fog, temperatures will\nremain above normal through Friday.\n\nA shortwave trough is passing from the eastern Great Lakes to\nNew England this morning. Only some high level clouds have been\naffecting our area, but now the thickest cloud shield is\ndeparting to the east. In its wake, some patchy fog is\ndeveloping, mainly in the typical valleys and other rural areas.\nSome locally dense fog is possible, but it appears unlikely it\nwill become widespread.\n\nAn upper level ridge will build over the area today while\nsurface high pressure remains offshore. Temperatures will climb\nfurther today, with most of the lower elevations reaching the\nlower 80s. Increasing low level winds will prevent full\ndecoupling tonight, resulting in most locations remaining in the\n60s for lows. Even with increasing high level clouds Friday, \nanother day with highs in the 80s is expected.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A stalling cold front may bring rain at times\nfrom Friday through Sunday.\n\nA cold front will approach from the northwest Friday afternoon,\neventually pushing south of the area Friday night. Showers will\nlikely reach western Maryland by midday, progresses\nsoutheastward during the afternoon and into the night. Despite \nthe warm temperatures, poor lapse rates aloft will limit \ninstability, so few if any thunderstorms are expected. A\nwesterly component to the flow could also result in downslope\ndrying, and many models show showers becoming more scattered in\nnature with eastward extent. Rainfall amounts with this round\nwill likely be less than a quarter inch for most locations.\n\nStrong high pressure will pass north of the area Saturday while\nthe front stalls across the southeastern states. Southern stream\nmoisture will likely overrun the boundary fairly far to the\nnorth, but there remains some uncertainty in the dividing line\nbetween rain and no rain. Overall, Saturday probably has a\nbetter chance of being dry with the high to the north. A wave of\nlow pressure will likely ride along the boundary Saturday night\ninto Sunday, and this may provide a higher chance of rain\nspreading northward into the area. In a reversal of last night,\nthe latest guidance has shifted back northward with rainfall\ncoverage during this period. In the end, the details are far\nfrom solidified. However, any rain that does occur will likely\nexit the area Sunday evening.\n\nBeing north of the boundary and potentially having a lot of\ncloud cover, high temperatures will likely remain in the 60s for\nmost areas over the weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Cooler and drier weather early next week with\npotential for frost and freeze west of the Blue Ridge Mountains.\n\nA second stronger cold front associated with a deepening trof\nsouth of James Bay will cross the area Monday morning. This\nappears to be a dry frontal passage, and temperatures could\nrebound back into the 70s as skies clear.\n\nA large area of high pressure will build from the Upper Midwest and \nGreat Lakes to the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic in the wake of the \nfront. This will bring at least a few days of dry weather and \nbelow normal temperatures. Highs will likely be in the 60s (50s \nmountains), with lows in the 40s to near 50 (30s mountains).\n\nAs high pressure moves overhead Monday night through Tuesday night, \nwinds will be light and skies will be clear. Given the cooler air in \nplace during this time and the favorable setup for radiational \ncooling, sheltered valleys and higher elevations near and west of \nthe Blue Ridge may be cold enough for frost formation. Some of the \ntypical cold sinks could drop to near freezing, as well. This is \nroughly 1 to 2 weeks earlier than average.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /07Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nPatchy fog is developing this morning, and has already been\naffecting CHO and MRB. It appears unlikely the other terminals\nare affected. Otherwise VFR conditions are expected into midday\nFriday with light southerly winds.\n\nA cold front will enter the area late Friday afternoon or\nevening. Some showers are possible along the front, but they\nwill likely be fairly light. A brief period of MVFR conditions\nis possible Friday night.\n\nThe front will stall to the south over the weekend, but its\nposition remains uncertain. Saturday has the greater chance of\nbeing dry, with a wave of low pressure potentially bringing rain\non Sunday. Any sub-VFR conditions would likely be limited to CHO\nSaturday, but could spread northward Sunday.\n\nVFR conditions are expected to prevail Monday into Tuesday, though \nsome early morning river valley fog is possible. Winds will be north \nwith an increase for a time in the wake of the weekend system. The \ntiming is a bit uncertain, but favors late Monday or early Tuesday \nwhen gusts of 20-25 kts are possible.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nSoutherly winds will uptick a little today into Friday. A Small\nCraft Advisory may be needed on the wider waters of the middle\nbay tonight into Friday morning, but confidence is lower since a\nsouthwesterly component to the flow would limit channeling\neffects. A cold front will push southward Friday night, with\nadvisories possible in the ensuing northerly flow through parts\nof Saturday. After that, winds may be relatively lighter until a\nsecond cold front pushes through on Monday.\n\nStrong northerly winds in the wake of this front will likely \nprompt SCAs Monday into Tuesday. A few gusts near gale force are\npossible if the stronger solutions verify.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nSome uncertainty continues to exist through Friday as tide \nlevels remain elevated and subtly fluctuating. These small \nchanges will make the difference in whether there is additional \nminor flooding. Alexandria, Dahlgren, Annapolis, and Havre de \nGrace have the greatest chance of reaching flood stage, although\nbased on current trends, Annapolis has the highest chance. The \nevening high tides are favored for any flooding since they are \nastronomically higher. A cold frontal passage should lead to \ndeclining water levels Friday night.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/DHOF\nAVIATION...ADS/DHOF\nMARINE...ADS/DHOF\n"},"forecast":{"t":1790844867,"days":{"2026-10-01":{"hi":85,"lo":65,"pop":3,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-02":{"hi":88,"lo":60,"pop":54,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Slight Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-10-03":{"hi":68,"lo":56,"pop":54,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-10-04":{"hi":65,"lo":57,"pop":64,"short":"Showers And Thunderstorms Likely"},"2026-10-05":{"hi":70,"lo":48,"pop":11,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-06":{"hi":61,"lo":45,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-07":{"hi":63,"lo":null,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1790841600,"n":48,"ceil":[19300,19300,19200,20100,21100,21400,null,null,null,null,23600,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,4900,4900,4900,5600,5600,5600,4500,4500]